  "body": "Closes #82.\n\nThe Epik app opens a window, and its first occupant is a chatbot. It greets with\n\"Hello, I'm Epik\" and holds a streaming conversation with a configured model. It\ndoes no git work and no coding — it exists so that the permanent infrastructure\nis forced into being by the simplest possible tenant: the app shell, the IPC\ndiscipline, streaming, model configuration, and secret storage.\n\nDesign: [`docs/design-history/2026-08-04-chatbot-walking-skeleton.md`](docs/design-history/2026-08-04-chatbot-walking-skeleton.md).\n\n## What is here\n\nFour sub-issues, in dependency order. The first three landed earlier on this\nbranch; the last one is the window.\n\n| | | |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| #83 | Cargo workspace: `epik`, `epik-app`, `epik-ui` | #87 |\n| #84 | Model-agnostic chat core in the `epik` library | #88 |\n| #86 | CI for the greenfield workspace with a live-model tier | #89 |\n| #85 | Chat window: Tauri host and Leptos frontend | #90 |\n\n**The library** (`crates/epik`) is everything Epik can do, synchronous and\nwasm-clean. `chat.rs` is the LLM client — `ChatModel` as the trait, `ChatEvent`\nas the vocabulary, `Conversation` as the owner of the canonical transcript —\nwith `Scripted` for tests and one hand-rolled `OpenAiCompatible` client whose\nwire format covers OpenAI, Ollama, Groq, Gemini, OpenRouter and Anthropic's\ncompatibility endpoint. `session.rs` is what a client opens: the config read,\nthe key resolved, the model built, the transcript kept. `event.rs` holds the\nvocabularies and the `Envelope` that carries a conversation id; `logging.rs`\nholds the sinks, one generic `Log<E>` serving every vocabulary; `ipc.rs` holds\nthe names both ends of the boundary agree on; `keystore.rs` keeps keys out of\nEpik and in the operating system.\n\n**The host** (`crates/epik-app`) is a Tauri window and nothing else. Three\ncommands, each one library call plus the two things Tauri needs and the library\nrefuses to know about — a blocking thread and a serializable error — and one\npump thread carrying the event channel to the webview. `try_lock` on the session\n*is* one-in-flight-turn-per-conversation. A refusal comes back as a command\nerror; a turn that broke mid-stream goes out as a `Failed` event. Two channels,\nnever mixed.\n\n**The frontend** (`crates/epik-ui`) is Leptos with Tailwind, compiled by Trunk.\nIts state is one `Pane`: a pure fold over the user's messages, the host's\nevents, and the results of the commands it sent — no Leptos, no IPC, no DOM, and\nso tested on the host target with a plain `cargo test`. Design tokens are\nderived from `brand.json`, both palettes exist from the first component, and a\ntest fails if either drifts from the brand.\n\n**CI** runs fmt, clippy at `-D warnings`, and the tests for the workspace and\nfor the frontend on two targets — plus a live tier that talks to a real model\nwith no secret anywhere: Ollama installed in the job, a sub-1B model cached as a\nblob, and `EPIK_REQUIRE_LIVE=1` so that a missing server fails the job rather\nthan skipping a tier that would look green forever.\n\n## The demo loop\n\nThe parent issue's acceptance was checked against a running window — the release\nbuild on a virtual display, Ollama behind it, driven with synthetic input.\n\n| Criterion | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Launch → the status bar names the active model | `smollm2:135m · ollama` |\n| \"Hi\" → the reply streams into the transcript as it generates | caret and disabled input while streaming, then the finished turn as markdown |\n| The theme toggle flips the whole window between light and dark | both palettes, including the light-mode accent and warning colours |\n| Killing the network mid-turn renders a `Failed` error card; the app stays responsive | red card in the transcript, input re-enabled, earlier turns intact |\n| Pointing the config at Ollama continues the same experience with zero code changes and zero dollars | the whole run above was Ollama |\n\nThe status bar also picked up Ollama's own token counts, so the cost-telemetry\npath is real rather than merely wired.\n\nRunning it found three defects that no test would have, all fixed here: a\nmissing Tauri capability file (which denied `core:event:listen` silently, so the\nwindow looked healthy and never answered anything), an undeclared\n`custom-protocol` feature (so a release build looked for the dev server), and an\nunreachable keyring refusing to open a session at all — on any machine with no\nsecret service, for Ollama, which wants no key.\n\n## Two things for a reviewer to weigh\n\n**Key resolution gained a third state.** The ADR says the order is\n`EPIK_API_KEY` → keyring → absent. It does not say what happens when the keyring\nitself will not answer, and the answer mattered: `Keys::resolve` now returns\n`Found`, `Absent` or `Unreachable(reason)`, and an unreachable keyring opens the\nsession anyway rather than refusing to start. The window still offers somewhere\nto paste a key and says on the card that a pasted one will not be kept. This is\na refinement of an accepted decision rather than a departure from one, but it is\nthe sort of thing the design history may want a line about.\n\n**`crates/epik-app/README.md` is new**, because three of the steps needed to run\nthe window are things nothing tells you. If a better home for that exists, it\nbelongs there.\n\n**Taste is unverified.** Whether the spacing, the weights and the two palettes\nare *right* is a judgement, and a headless build has no eyes. That is the part\nthis review is for.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
